rness advertises on `session/new` (`agent_sdk/backends_models.py`). Effort takes the same channel under the default `effort` id. The option is per model: opencode builds it from the model's own variants (low, medium and high on an OpenAI-compatible reasoning model) and advertises none on a model without them, so the advertised option answers whether a level applies (`ACP_BACKENDS_EFFORT_FROM_ADVERTISED_OPTION`) and a level the model does not offer is hidden. A `model` write resets the variant to the new model's first one, so a live `set_model` re-applies the slot's level (`test/fixtures/acp_frames/opencode/effort-live.jsonl`) | over the wire, not in a spec: `session/set_config_option("model", …)`, whose vocabulary is the `provider/model` list pi resolves from its own `models.json` and advertises on `session/new` (`ollama/llama3.2:3b` for a local model). Effort takes the same channel, under pi's own spelling: the option advertised beside `model` is `thought_level` (off, minimal, low, medium, high, plus xhigh and max where the model maps them), which `EFFORT_CONFIG_OPTION_IDS` records and `effort_config_option_id` answers. pi spells every shared level as Crew does, so a level the current model lacks is hidden rather than folded, with one exception: a saved `max` on a model that offers `xhigh` but not `max` is written as `xhigh` (`EFFORT_CONFIG_OPTION_ABSENT_FOLDS`). Either way the push writes only an advertised value (`pi/effort-live.jsonl`). Whether a level applies at all is answered by that advertised option too, not by Crew's model registry (`ACP_BACKENDS_EFFORT_FROM_ADVERTISED_OPTION`): the ids here are the operator's own, so the registry recognises none of them and would report no effort control for every ordinary session (`agent_sdk/backends_models.py`) | over the wire, not in a spec: `session/set_config_option("model", …)`, whose vocabulary is the `provider` and `model` selects the harness advertises on `session/new` (`agent_sdk/backends_models.py`). Effort takes the same channel, under goose's own spelling `thinking_effort` (`EFFORT_CONFIG_OPTION_IDS`). The option is per model: a thinking model is offered off, low, medium, high and max, and a model with no extended thinking is offered `off` alone, which `AcpProvider.supports_effort` reads as no effort control rather than a dropdown whose writes all read back `off`. goose re-advertises the option after every write and model switch, so the advertised option answers whether a level applies (`ACP_BACKENDS_EFFORT_FROM_ADVERTISED_OPTION`); `xhigh` has no counterpart and is hidden (`agent_sdk/backends_models.py`) | over the wire, not in a spec: `session/set_config_option("model", …)`, whose vocabulary is the OPAQUE JSON-encoded `[provider, model]` pairs it advertises on `session/new`. Effort takes the same channel and is advertised as `reasoning_effort`, not `effort`, which is what `EFFORT_CONFIG_OPTION_IDS` records and `effort_config_option_id` answers (`agent_sdk/backends_models.py`) | none advertised on the recording account (Auto only), so the session runs the harness's own choice |
